{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And Pharaoh called unto Moses.</b> Even though in all the plagues he called to Moses and to Aaron, nevertheless now he called none other than Moses alone, for he knew that when Moses was born the entire house was filled with light, and he thought that perhaps this was a sign concerning the future — that he would possess the special property of bringing light into the place of darkness." "<b>Only your flocks and your herds shall be set apart.</b> If their flocks and herds are set apart, what then will they slaughter? And the word 'only' is superfluous. And why did he call them here 'flocks and herds,' and afterward say 'and also our livestock shall go with us'? And furthermore it is difficult: why did he insert 'flocks and herds' between the attached words, for he should have said 'Go, serve YHWH; your little ones also shall go with you; only your flocks and herds shall be set apart' — for it would not be fitting after 'shall be set apart' to say 'also your little ones shall go,' and the word 'also' is superfluous. And furthermore it is difficult what he said: ״גַּם אַתָּה תִּתֵּן בְּיָדֵנוּ זְבָחִים וְעֹלוֹת״ (Exodus 10:25) — if this 'also' comes to include the livestock of Israel, then why did he go back and say ״וְגַם מִקְנֵנוּ יֵלֵךְ עִמָּנוּ״ (Exodus 10:26)? Has he not already included it in the word 'also'? And according to what appears, 'our livestock' means camels and donkeys, for without doubt Israel also had camels and donkeys, and concerning them he said 'and also our livestock shall go with us' — for even though they are not fit to be offered as a sacrifice, nevertheless ״מִמֶּנּוּ נִקַּח לַעֲבֹד אֶת ה׳ אֱלֹהֵינוּ״ (Exodus 10:26) — that is to say, from them we shall take and sell them and purchase with their proceeds flocks and herds for the service of YHWH. Therefore it says 'from them we shall take to serve,' and it should have said 'for from them we shall serve'; rather, he used the language of purchase and acquisition, and if so, perforce this livestock speaks of animals not fit for sacrifice." "But in order to settle all the difficulties, I say that Israel acquired much livestock beyond those flocks and herds that they already had, for they knew they would go to the wilderness, which is not a place for livestock. And Pharaoh thought that the reason they acquired them was in order to slaughter them to YHWH, so that they would not need to take from their own houses a bull or from their folds he-goats. And this is the sense of 'our livestock' — that is, the flocks and herds that they acquired now. And therefore Pharaoh said 'only your flocks and herds shall be set apart,' and with the word 'only' he diminished the livestock of their acquisition that would go with them for slaughter — not only that, which obviously must go, for there is no sacrifice without them, but also 'your little ones,' who are not needed for the service but only for the sake of joy, shall go regardless. And Moses said: not only ״צֹאנֵנוּ וּבְקָרֵנוּ״ that we already have — which obviously must go, for how is it possible that we would not take to slaughter to YHWH from the firstborn of our flocks and herds that we already possess, for it is not the way of honor to purchase from the market to slaughter to YHWH and not take from the folds, from the choicest flocks and herds that we already have — but even ״גַּם אַתָּה תִּתֵּן בְּיָדֵנוּ זְבָחִים וְעֹלוֹת.״ And if you will say: if so, presumably your intention is to offer the choicest, which means the flocks and herds that were raised on your feed; and if so, that which you purchased you intend to keep for yourselves, and if so at the very least that livestock which you purchased should be set apart here, since your intention is not to offer from it — to this he said ״גַּם מִקְנֵנוּ יֵלֵךְ עִמָּנוּ״ etc., for we did not acquire them in order to keep them for ourselves, but rather because ״אֲנַחְנוּ לֹא נֵדַע מַה נַּעֲבֹד אֶת ה׳״ — according to Rashi's explanation, that perhaps He will ask for more than what is in our hands. And with this all the difficulties are resolved." "<b>Also your little ones shall go with you.</b> He made the little ones secondary to them, but Moses said the reverse of this: ״בְּבָנֵינוּ וּבִבְנוֹתֵינוּ נֵלֵךְ״ (Exodus 10:9) — behold, they made themselves secondary even to the little ones. For Moses, from the abundance of humility that was in him, made himself secondary to all of them — to young men and elders, to sons and daughters — all of them are principal in the service of HaShem, blessed be He, and we shall go along with them, since we are secondary to them. And what he said, ״בְּצֹאנֵנוּ וּבִבְקָרֵנוּ נֵלֵךְ״ (Exodus 10:9) — because they go to be slaughtered for the service of YHWH, it is obvious that we are secondary to them, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Genesis Rabbah 33:1) on the verse ״אָדָם וּבְהֵמָה תּוֹשִׁיעַ ה׳״ (Psalms 36:7) — 'man' by merit of 'beast.' But Pharaoh, out of concern for honor, could not possibly make them secondary to the little ones; therefore he said 'also your little ones shall go with you,' making them principal and the little ones secondary to them. And what Moses said, ״וְגַם מִקְנֵנוּ יֵלֵךְ עִמָּנוּ״ (Exodus 10:26) — behold, he made the livestock secondary, for here he speaks of acquired livestock, and it is the way of the whole that their property is secondary to them and they rule over their acquisitions — unlike the way of the deficient, whose acquisition of property rules over them and drags them wherever it desires, as we discussed at length on this matter in the portion of וַיִּשְׁלַח on the verse ״יֶשׁ לִי רָב״ (Genesis 33:9) — see there. Whether according to the first explanation we mentioned above, that ״גַּם מִקְנֵנוּ״ refers to camels and donkeys, or according to the second explanation, that it speaks of animals they acquired for their needs — in any case it is all acquired property, and it is not the way of the whole to make themselves secondary and their property principal. But above it was said ״בְּצֹאנֵנוּ וּבִבְקָרֵנוּ נֵלֵךְ״ — going to the service of YHWH to be slaughtered — they made themselves secondary, as stated."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:19:35.253527805Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}